---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import api from '@busy-office/ui/api';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/* Generated, never hand-listed: which components ship an explicit
   forced-colors rule. The prose used to enumerate them and had drifted to
   10 of 15. pageSlug is the same alias map the page-shape gate uses — a CSS
   directory name is not always a page slug (skeleton is documented on
   state-patterns), and a generated link that 404s is worse than a
   hand-written list; the link checker caught exactly that here. */
const forcedColors = Object.entries(api.components)
  .filter(([, v]) => v.forcedColors)
  .map(([name]) => ({ name, slug: api.pageSlug?.[name] ?? name }))
  .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const liveRegions = `<!-- ARRIVES after load (htmx swap, fetch, client validation):
     a live region is right. Severity picks WHICH one. -->
<div class="bo-alert bo-alert--danger" role="alert">Posting failed.</div>
<div class="bo-alert bo-alert--success" role="status">Saved.</div>

<!-- PRESENT at load (server re-rendered the form after a failed POST):
     no live region. Wire it to the field, and move focus to a summary. -->
<div class="bo-form-field">
  <label class="bo-label" for="cc">Cost center</label>
  <input class="bo-input" id="cc" aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="cc-err">
  <span class="bo-form-field__message" id="cc-err">Unknown cost center code</span>
</div>

<!-- The one shape that is BOTH: present in the markup, hidden until it
     applies. Unhiding it is the arrival, so the role is correct. -->
<div class="bo-alert bo-alert--danger" data-validation-summary-box hidden role="alert">`;
const twoChannel = `<!-- BOTH channels, every state signal: -->
<li class="bo-timeline__step" data-state="rejected">
  <span class="bo-timeline__marker" aria-hidden="true">✕</span>   <!-- visible, non-color -->
  <div class="bo-timeline__title">
    <span class="bo-visually-hidden">Rejected: </span>Finance release <!-- programmatic -->
  </div>
</li>`;
---
<Gallery title="Accessibility model">
  <p class="demo-note">
    The framework's rule, learned the hard way across three design reviews:
    <strong>never-color-alone has two audiences.</strong> Every state signal ships a
    visible non-color cue (glyph, text) for sighted colorblind users AND a
    programmatic channel (visually-hidden text, ARIA state) for assistive tech.
    Satisfying one audience is a defect.
  </p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The two-channel pattern</h2>
    <pre><code>{twoChannel}</code></pre>
    <p>Same pattern everywhere: stat deltas pair a visible <code>⚠</code> with hidden
    "increase, worse," text; error rows pair a background tint with an inset border
    and expect a badge/message in the row; badges are decoration — the status
    <em>text</em> is the signal.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo" id="live-regions">
    <h2>Live regions: arrival decides, severity only refines</h2>
    <p>A live region announces <em>changes</em>. Content that is already in the
    HTML when the page is parsed has not changed, so screen readers commonly do
    not announce it — and where one does, it interrupts whatever the user was
    reading. <strong>So the first question is never "how bad is this?" — it is
    "was this here when the page loaded?"</strong></p>
    <pre><code>{liveRegions}</code></pre>
    <p>Two questions, asked in this order:</p>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Does it arrive after load?</strong> No → no live region at all.
      Yes → live region.</li>
      <li><strong>Only then, how urgent?</strong> An error interrupts:
      <code>role="alert"</code> (assertive). Anything else waits its turn:
      <code>role="status"</code> (polite).</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This page states the rule because the framework used to carry
    <strong>two</strong> of them. <code>alert.css</code> and the alerts page said
    the role was picked by severity; <code>form-field.css</code> and the table
    below said it was picked by arrival. Both were describing something real —
    severity is the right answer to question 2 — but it was being used to answer
    question 1, which is how eight static demos ended up shipping an assertive
    live region for text that was never going to arrive.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The ERP case that makes this matter: a server
    re-renders a form with errors after a failed POST. The error genuinely is new
    information, but it arrives as a <em>page load</em>, not a DOM mutation — so
    the mechanism is focus management and a summary the user lands on, which is
    what <a href={base + '/patterns/validation-summary'}>validation summary</a>
    is for. Enforced by <code>check:live-regions</code>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The framework's job vs yours</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">The framework guarantees</th><th scope="col">Your markup must supply</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>AA contrast for every token-role pair, both themes — <a href={base + '/reference/tokens'}>computed and CI-gated</a></td><td><code>aria-invalid</code> + <code>aria-describedby</code> on form errors (+ <code>role="alert"</code> if dynamic)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Visible <code>:focus-visible</code> rings everywhere; Tab loop in dialogs; roving tabindex in tabs</td><td><code>aria-labelledby</code> on dialogs; <code>aria-label</code> on icon-only buttons and search inputs</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Heights as minimums — <strong>WCAG 1.4.12 verified in CI</strong> on every page at both widths, comparing clipping before and after the user spacing override; SC 2.5.8 target size verified in CI by the real spec test (a 24px circle on each undersized control must reach no other target) — met via the spacing exception, NOT a 24px floor; state styled from ARIA (<code>aria-sort</code>, <code>aria-current</code>, <code>aria-busy</code>)</td><td>The two-channel state text (<code>Completed:</code>/<code>Rejected:</code>…); <code>role="list"</code> on styled lists; <code>name</code>/<code>value</code> on selection checkboxes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Live "n selected" count via <code>initDataTables()</code>; reduced-motion zeroing on all animations</td><td>Keeping compaction-hidden columns reachable (the data-table page's reflow requirement, WCAG 1.4.10); never <code>user-scalable=no</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Forced colors (Windows High Contrast)</h2>
    <p>Under <code>forced-colors: active</code> the system repaints text,
    borders, and controls in its own palette — most components survive
    automatically because state rides on text, glyphs, and borders (the
    two-channel rule again). Explicit <code>@media (forced-colors: active)</code>
    rules exist exactly where a state's only visible channel was a background
    or a mask — <strong>icons</strong> (mask repainted <code>CanvasText</code>),
    <strong>skeleton</strong> (bars become outlined boxes instead of
    vanishing), <strong>combobox</strong> (active option repaints with
    <code>SelectedItem</code>), <strong>stepper</strong> (current marker gains
    a ring) — across these {forcedColors.length} components:</p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      {forcedColors.map((c) => (
        <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={`${base}/components/${c.slug}`}>{c.name}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">That list is <strong>generated</strong> from
    the shipped CSS (<code>api.json</code>), not maintained by hand — it had
    drifted to 10 of {forcedColors.length} while still claiming to be
    exhaustive, which is exactly the failure mode generating it removes.
    Each rule is verified under CDP forced-colors emulation against a
    control run in normal mode, so a rule that no longer changes anything
    is caught rather than assumed.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Honest status</h2>
    <p>Contrast is verified per token pair in both themes on every build. Screen-reader
    runtime passes (VoiceOver/NVDA verbalization) sit on an open ledger in the public
    <a href="https://github.com/Busy-Office/busy-office-ui/tree/main/.roundtable">review trail</a> —
    designed to WCAG 2.2 AA, with the unverified parts named rather than claimed. The
    <a href={base + '/reference/acr'}>Accessibility Conformance Report</a> turns this
    into a per-criterion table, generated from the same evidence.</p>
  </section>
  <Related links={[["/reference/tokens", "Contrast tables"], ["/concepts/density", "Density"], ["/reference/acr", "Accessibility Conformance Report"]]} />
</Gallery>
